I could not refute this finding. At d45368b, the money check and the writer look at different strings. In src/RugsManagment.Application/Features/Rugs/RugFieldWriter.cs:108, EnsureMayWrite tests the raw dictionary keys with RugFieldKeys.IsMoney. That function (src/RugsManagment.Application/Fields/RugFieldKeys.cs:77-78) is an exact ordinal HashSet lookup plus StartsWith("purchase."). Neither matches " rug.targetSalePrice" or "\tpurchase.price". ApplyAsync then calls rawKey.Trim() at line 125 and switches on the trimmed key:
- line 140 sets TargetSalePrice;
- lines 144-145 put the key in plan.Purchase;
- PlanPurchaseAsync (lines 647-709) matches the trimmed key against the category seed key;
- ApplyPurchaseAsync (lines 773-817) adds, updates or removes the line.

Neither of these methods, nor ReadTargetSalePrice (line 406), checks money.CanSeeMoney. Only ApplyLegacyPurchaseCostAsync (line 254) has that guard. The service calls the same raw-key check: RugManagementService.cs:141 (create) and :234 (update), then ApplyAsync at :155 and :253. RugsApiController.Update and Create (RugsApiController.cs:60-69) sit under the Floor policy from ApiControllerBase, so an Operator can reach both. The request's Values is IReadOnlyDictionary<string, JsonElement?> (RugDtos.cs:201, 251). The Web and Application projects have no custom JSON options or converters, and System.Text.Json keeps dictionary keys exactly as sent. A Business-only 403 check is therefore skipped by a key with leading whitespace.
